
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">&#8220;Driven by the de facto dominance of English, AI multilingual alignment shows a form of cross-linguistic deference, a systematic tendency to resolve cross-linguistic disagreement by treating the English judgment as authoritative, rather than acknowledging the natural cross-linguistic inconsistency of human natural language.&#8221;</p>



<span id="more-27268"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That&#8217;s from the &#8220;<a href="https://sites.unica.it/ljs/cs/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Cagliari ​​Statement on Linguistic​ and Conceptual Justice in AI​</a>&#8220;, which objects to correcting such inconsistencies&#8221; in ways that &#8220;suppress legitimate semantic plurality and progressively establish English (spoken natively by only about 6% of the world’s population) as the de facto standard for what meanings should remain equivalent across languages.&#8221; </p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="635" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-1024x635.png" alt="" class="wp-image-27270" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-1024x635.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-300x186.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-768x476.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-1536x952.png 1536w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-2048x1269.png 2048w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ballero-antonio-Mattino-di-marzo-250x155.png 250w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Antonio Ballero, &#8220;Mattino di marzo&#8221;</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rather, according to the statement, &#8220;language models require language-specific alignment, grounded in rich data on how ordinary speakers actually use their words in each language.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The statement, which is currently open for signatures, includes distinct recommendations for AI developers, researchers in linguistics and philosophy, institutions and funders, and standard-setting bodies, among others.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It was originally drafted by <a href="https://caglacimendereli.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Çağla Çimendereli</a> (Old Dominion), <a href="https://contesi.info/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Filippo Contesi</a> (Cagliari), and <a href="https://philpeople.org/profiles/masaharu-mizumoto">Masaharu Mizumoto</a> (Japan Advanced Institute of Science and Technology) and worked on during the Linguistic Justice Society Satellite Workshop at the <a href="https://sites.unica.it/6euroxphi/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">6th European Experimental Philosophy Conference</a>. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The statement provides some background on the issue:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>While the capabilities of current frontier AI models have shown remarkable progress in areas such as mathematical ability, their semantic competence in natural languages—including, in particular, lower-resource languages—fails to accurately reflect basic human semantic intuitions (i.e., how ordinary speakers actually use their words in diverse contexts). This gap is what this statement addresses. </em></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>Multilingual AI systems now answer questions about foreign facts, reports, opinions, etc. in local languages without explicitly indicating that translation mediates the response. Moreover, because these systems acquire meaning from patterns of usage, they inherit the semantic norms of the larger corpora they are trained on—which are predominantly English. We therefore hold that decisions about how such systems behave across languages are not merely technical, but normative.  </em></p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The full statement is <a href="https://sites.unica.it/ljs/cs/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">here</a>.</p>



